","nucleus_padj":6.25392e-09,"nucleus_log2fc":0.218109,"nucleus_score":6.13078,"pct_ad":0.42117,"pct_control":0.380415,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.479905,"donor_log2fc":0.158269,"donor_lfcse":0.0832969,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.0548614,"degas_log2fc":0.0866679,"degas_padj":0.02515},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_symbol":"ATP6V0E1","gene_symbol_current":"ATP6V0E1","gene_id":"ENSG00000113732","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.853735,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0195314,"nucleus_score":0.295695,"pct_ad":0.304354,"pct_control":0.301101,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.999342,"donor_log2fc":0.0303745,"donor_lfcse":0.0963499,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":3.43204e-06,"degas_log2fc":0.299812,"degas_padj":6.20635e-09},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_symbol":"ATP6V0E1","gene_symbol_current":"ATP6V0E1","gene_id":"ENSG00000113732","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.190515,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0447711,"nucleus_score":1.41845,"pct_ad":0.267628,"pct_control":0.263797,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.887141,"donor_log2fc":0.0533626,"donor_lfcse":0.0664339,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.0116798,"degas_log2fc":0.0959379,"degas_padj":3.52923e-05}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_id":"ENSG00000113732","module":"turquoise","kme":0.116884,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.680759,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_id":"ENSG00000113732","module":"turquoise","kme":0.10309,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.842971,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000113732","entrez_id":["8992"],"uniprot":["O15342"],"ncbi_summary":"This gene encodes a component of vacuolar ATPase (V-ATPase), a multisubunit enzyme that mediates acidification of eukaryotic intracellular organelles. V-ATPase dependent organelle acidification is necessary for such intracellular processes as protein sorting, zymogen activation, receptor-mediated endocytosis, and synaptic vesicle proton gradient generation. V-ATPase is composed of a cytosolic V1 domain and a transmembrane V0 domain. The V1 domain consists of three A and three B subunits, two G subunits plus the C, D, E, F, and H subunits. The V1 domain contains the ATP catalytic site. The V0 domain consists of five different subunits: a, c, c', c', and d. Additional isoforms of many of the V1 and V0 subunit proteins are encoded by multiple genes or alternatively spliced transcript variants. This encoded protein is possibly part of the V0 subunit. Since two nontranscribed pseudogenes have been found in dog, it is possible that the localization to chromosome 2 for this gene by radiation hybrid mapping is representing a pseudogene. Genomic mapping puts the chromosomal location on 5q35.3. [provided by RefSeq, Jul 2008].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"O15342","text":"Subunit of the V0 complex of vacuolar(H+)-ATPase (V-ATPase), a multisubunit enzyme composed of a peripheral complex (V1) that hydrolyzes ATP and a membrane integral complex (V0) that translocates protons (PubMed:33065002). V-ATPase is responsible for acidifying and maintaining the pH of intracellular compartments and in some cell types, is targeted to the plasma membrane, where it promotes acidification of the extracellular environment (By similarity). The V-ATPase complex also acts as an activator for mTORC1 on lysosomal membrane by promoting the guanine nucleotide exchange factor (GEF) of the Ragulator complex, thereby enabling mTORC1 recruitment (PubMed:22053050)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}